  slide "AIPerf: the worker process" {
    eyebrow: "AIPerf architecture"
    title: "A worker is a real OS process, not a green thread"
    lede: "AIPerf's MultiProcessServiceManager spawns each worker as a multiprocessing.Process (fork or spawn context) — a genuinely separate OS process with its own PID, memory space, and event loop."

    narration: "Where Locust's virtual users are cheap cooperative greenlets sharing one process, AIPerf's workers are heavyweight by comparison and genuinely parallel: each one is spawned via Python's multiprocessing module as its own OS process, with its own PID visible in a process listing, its own memory space, and its own asyncio event loop. AIPerf isn't alone in spawning multiple processes — several other AIPerf services (the timing manager, dataset manager, record processors) are separate OS processes too, communicating over a ZMQ message bus rather than shared memory or function calls."

    term: {
      word: "multiprocessing.Process"
      meaning: "Python's standard mechanism for spawning a genuinely separate OS process (via fork or spawn) with its own interpreter, memory space, and GIL — as opposed to a thread or greenlet, which share the parent process's memory and, for threads, its GIL."
    }

    points: [
      "AIPerf worker processes are multiprocessing.Process instances — real PIDs, real separate memory.",
      "Not just HTTP workers: timing_manager, dataset_manager, record_processor_* are separate OS processes too.",
      "Inter-process coordination happens over a ZMQ message bus, not shared memory.",
      "Evidence: aiperf/controller/multiprocess_service_manager.py — `Process | SpawnProcess | ForkProcess`."
    ]

    caption: "Every AIPerf worker is a real, separately-scheduled OS process."

    render: @scene {
      sdk.Header(
        id = "s4-hdr",
        title = "PROCESS, NOT GREENLET",
        caption = "Every AIPerf worker is a real, separately-scheduled OS process.",
        x = 18, y = 16, width = 664, height = 44,
        surfaceRole = theme(surface.secondary),
        inkRole = theme(ink.secondary)
      )
      sdk.Panel(
        id = "s4-w1",
        title = "worker_a1",
        detail = "PID 1001 · own event loop",
        x = 40, y = 100, width = 180, height = 90,
        surfaceRole = theme(surface.elevated),
        strokeRole = theme(accent.primary)
      )
      sdk.Panel(
        id = "s4-w2",
        title = "worker_b2",
        detail = "PID 1002 · own event loop",
        x = 260, y = 100, width = 180, height = 90,
        surfaceRole = theme(surface.elevated),
        strokeRole = theme(accent.primary)
      )
      sdk.Panel(
        id = "s4-w3",
        title = "worker_c3",
        detail = "PID 1003 · own event loop",
        x = 480, y = 100, width = 180, height = 90,
        surfaceRole = theme(surface.elevated),
        strokeRole = theme(accent.primary)
      )
      sdk.Panel(
        id = "s4-other",
        title = "timing_manager · dataset_manager · record_processor_*",
        detail = "also separate OS processes",
        x = 100, y = 230, width = 460, height = 70,
        surfaceRole = theme(surface.elevated),
        strokeRole = theme(accent.tertiary)
      )
      sdk.Note(
        id = "s4-note",
        text = "No shared memory between processes — coordination happens over ZMQ",
        x = 100, y = 330, width = 460, height = 30
      )
      sdk.Signal(
        id = "s4-motion",
        from = { nodeId: "s4-w1", anchor: "center" },
        to = { nodeId: "s4-w3", anchor: "center" },
        opacity = 0.4,
        strokeWidth = 2,
        label = "independent PIDs",
        style = { stroke: theme(accent.primary) }
      )

      timeline main {
        at 0 reveal s4-hdr duration 260
        after s4-hdr reveal s4-w1 duration 240
        after s4-w1 reveal s4-w2 duration 240
        after s4-w2 reveal s4-w3 duration 240
        after s4-w3 reveal s4-other duration 260
        after s4-other reveal s4-note duration 260
